is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: mswitch0
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Campus 1 multilayer switch mswitch0 (routing control-plane/FRR on the SVIs vlan10/20/30). Mechanism: Campus 1 hosts use mswitch0 (.254) as default gateway. When they send traffic to Campus 2 subnets (10.5.0.0/24, 10.6.0.0/24), mswitch0 has no route and immediately returns ICMP Destination Net Unreachable, so end hosts report Campus 2 as unreachable. This is visible from pc5→pc1 ping showing “From 172.16.10.254 Destination Net Unreachable.” Localization: mswitch0. Its routing table only contains connected networks (10.2.2.0/24, 172.16.10/20/30) with no learned routes to 10.5/10.6, and its FRR service is down (frr.service Active: inactive (dead)) with no /etc/frr/frr.conf present. Meanwhile, the router side is healthy: routers 16/7/17 have OSPF routes for 10.5/10.6, and router16 has an OSPF neighbor over 172.30.1.0/24, indicating the inter-campus core is intact. Root cause: FRR/OSPF is not running or configured on mswitch0, so Campus 1 SVIs are not advertised upstream and mswitch0 does not learn Campus 2 routes. Corrective fix: Restore dynamic routing on mswitch0. Create an FRR OSPF config advertising 10.2.2.0/24 (active) and vlan10/20/30 (passive), e.g., router ospf with network 10.2.2.0/24 area 0 and passive-interface vlan10/vlan20/vlan30; then enable and start frr.service. This will establish routing with router16 (10.2.2.1) so mswitch0 learns 10.5/10.6 and advertises 172.16.10/20/30, restoring Campus 1 ↔ Campus 2 reachability. === END ===